# TypeScript Standards

## No `any`

Never use `any`. If a type is unknown, use `unknown` and narrow it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function parseResponse(data: any) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
function parseResponse(data: unknown) {
    if (!isProgression(data)) throw new Error("Unexpected response shape")
    return data
}
```

## No type assertions without justification

`as Foo` silently discards type safety. Only use it when you have verified the shape and cannot express it otherwise. Always leave a comment.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const chord = raw as ChordData

// ✅ GOOD
// Response shape is validated by isChordData() above
const chord = raw as ChordData
```

## `type` vs `interface`

Use `type` for data shapes and unions. Use `interface` only when you need declaration merging (rare).

```typescript
// ✅ data shape → type
type ChordData = {
    root: string
    quality: string
    notes: string[]
}

// ✅ union → type
type PlaybackState = "idle" | "playing" | "paused"
```

## Shared types

All shared types live in `types/chord.ts`. Never re-define a type locally if it already exists there. Import it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — re-defining locally
type Chord = { root: string; quality: string }

// ✅ GOOD
import type { ChordData } from "@/types/chord"
```

## Import types

Use `import type` when importing only type definitions. This is erased at build time and keeps runtime imports clean.

```typescript
import type { ChordData, Progression } from "@/types/chord"
```
